{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261026T120949Z-e0257509", "ts": "2026-10-26T12:14:26Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.8.0-dev", "branch": "fix/1072-final-review-chunks"}, "body": {"base_commit": "4716fcc3e78f1ce565689714d2166cf892b1f351", "base_tree": "c0e4775b3fe6c30fd6302f064651b391de02473b", "head_tree": "66589cc7651dfa681badedf6000d0de617175ed9", "head_commit": "f0a49297df38c0fec91ce44cad61ffd2bc73a64f", "dispatch_commit": "f0a49297df38c0fec91ce44cad61ffd2bc73a64f", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5[1m]"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5[1m]"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5[1m]"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/methodology/planning.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_ephemeral_worktree.py", "tests/test_plan_retract.py", "tests/test_plan_status.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/boundary-patterns.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-final-review-chunks.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/security-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/final-review-chunks.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/methodology/planning.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_ephemeral_worktree.py", "tests/test_plan_retract.py", "tests/test_plan_status.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/boundary-patterns.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-final-review-chunks.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/security-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/final-review-chunks.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "A verify-resolutions pass re-certifies a chunk the builder retracted from a blocked review", "recommendation": "Why it broke: `critic_consolidate._proposed_chunks` builds the verify-resolutions proposal from `critic_mode.chain_named_chunks(chain)`, which is the raw `fact_chunks` of the newest naming fact on the chain, and it never asks `plan_status.retractions`. The sequence: R1 (a `final` that wrongly names 01-07) carries a blocking finding. Following `retraction_target`'s own advice for an uncertified chunk ('name one with `--fact` to withdraw it before it can certify'), the builder runs `plan retract 03 --fact R1`. The verify pass V1 anchored to R1 then proposes R1's full set. With no `--chunks` given, V1's fact records 01-07, and once V1 is clear `chunk_states` reads 03 as done by V1. The pair (V1, 03) was never retracted, so the retraction is silently undone by the same declare-nothing default that #1072 fixed. Scope: class. Every proposal or certification set inherited from a fact's chunk list without passing through `chunk_states` is a member. Today that is the verify branch of `_proposed_chunks`; grep `chain_named_chunks` and `fact_chunks` callers for others. Fix it in one owner: drop retracted (naming-fact, chunk) pairs from the inherited set, for example with a `plan_status` helper that both use. Add a test that retracts a pair of a blocked review, dispatches verify-resolutions, and asserts the retracted chunk is neither proposed nor certified. Red-verify it against the current code.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "Retracting a fact on a chunk whose Status box is still ticked reports success while the chunk stays done", "recommendation": "Why it broke: `chunk_states` reads ticked `## Status` boxes (MG1) after the import loop and checks no import or retraction fact first. `plan import-status --apply` keeps the block unless `--remove-block` is passed, and that flag is opt-in. So for a migrated-but-kept box, `plan retract 01` (fact omitted) targets the `chunk-imported` fact, writes the retraction and exits 0 with `action: retracted`. The box then re-certifies 01 in memory, and the output is only `chunk 01 now reads: done`, with no 'certified by' and no 'untick the box'. A later `import-status --apply` sees exactly the 'done, imported, no fact' state it converts, so it mints a fresh import fact that the retraction does not name. A review fact retracted with `--fact` on a ticked-box chunk behaves the same way. The `box-import` refusal fires only when `--fact` is omitted and no fact certifies. `test_a_retracted_import_falls_to_the_next_answer` pins only the no-box case: its docstring says 'with no box left'. Scope: instance. Either have `retraction_target` refuse `box-import` (untick the box) whenever the chunk's box is still ticked, or write the retraction and have the after-line say the box still certifies it and must be unticked. Pin the chosen path at the CLI with a ticked-box fixture.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "tests/test_plan_retract.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "Re-running the same `plan retract` without `--fact` exits 1, not the documented idempotent exit 0", "recommendation": "After a successful `plan retract 02 --reason X`, the identical command finds 02 open with no certifying fact and refuses `not-certified` (exit 1). The api-contract entry's 'Exit 0 written or already retracted' and the docstring's 'already retracted (nothing written)' are reachable only when `--fact` is passed again. `test_a_rerun_writes_nothing_again` re-runs with `--fact R1`, so the literal re-run is untested. A retrying script or agent reads exit 1 as failure. The refusal text is accurate, which is why this is a note. Scope: instance. Either answer `already` when the fact is omitted and the chunk's only naming facts are retracted, or have the contract say the idempotent exit needs `--fact`.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "tests/test_plan_retract.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "note", "title": "The unscoped subject fallback now feeds certification, not just a table tag", "recommendation": "`_walk_branch` filters subjects by the plan's conventional-commit scope only when at least one subject carries it. Otherwise every subject on `base..branch` counts, including another scope's `feat(other): Chunk 03`. That rule was tolerable for `built_report`, and the plan says 'a table tag can afford that error'. Through `subject_named_chunks` it now decides what a whole-plan review certifies when `--chunks` is omitted. This is the same reasoning the plan used to reject `built_report`'s path rule. The case is narrow: it needs a branch whose commits never use the plan's scope and which carries another plan's chunk-naming subjects. Scope: instance. Decide whether the proposal should take only scoped subjects when the plan declares a `scope:`, and record the decision either way.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "A mid-plan final's subject rule reaches the whole branch, not the review's interval, so it re-certifies a retracted chunk it never saw", "recommendation": "Why it broke: `propose_chunks` gets only `files_changed`, but `subject_named_chunks` walks the plan's whole `base..branch`. So a whole-plan proposal names every open chunk any commit subject names, whatever interval the review saw. For `cumulative` that is harmless, because its interval is merge-base...HEAD. A `final` reviews a delta, though. Since `chunk-retracted` is observational, the retracted review stays clean coverage, and the next `final` starts after it. Example: retract built Chunk 01's wrong certification, build 02, run a mid-plan `final`. The proposal is [01, 02], and with `--chunks` omitted the fact certifies 01 again over a diff that does not contain 01's work. The plan's own Success line says a mid-plan final \"names only the chunks its interval built\", and the code does not do that. Fix (scope: instance): pass the dispatch's commit range (`base_reviewed..head`) to `propose_chunks`, and walk only those subjects for `final`. Keep the branch walk for `cumulative`, whose interval is the branch. Pin it with a test: retract a subject-named chunk, then a mid-plan `final` whose interval excludes it must not propose it. If the branch-wide reach is intended, record that decision in the plan beside the Success line it contradicts.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "plan retract copies a hand-rolled archived-plan check that misses nested archives; the one home already exists", "recommendation": "Why it broke: `_cmd_plan_retract` copied `_cmd_plan_import_status`'s inline check, `under_artifacts[:1] == (\"archive\",)`. That check catches only a top-level `.prawduct/artifacts/archive/`. But `plan_archive` (line ~377) moves a plan in a subdirectory to `<plan dir>/archive/<name>`, and `plan_index` (line ~436) documents that nested shape as real. `work_unit.is_live_plan_file`, whose docstring says it is \"The one home of 'is this plan live'\", tests `plan_index.ARCHIVE_DIR_NAME in rel.parts`, and so does `plan_mint`. So `plan retract --plan .prawduct/artifacts/plans/x/archive/build-plan-x.md` is not refused `archived-plan`, and it reopens a chunk of a plan whose chunk states the guard's own message calls history. Scope: class, two members, both in `plugin/bin/prawduct-hook`: retract (new) and import-status (copied from). Search for `relative_to(` beside `\"archive\"`. Close it through one owner: both ops ask the shared archived predicate, or one `ARCHIVE_DIR_NAME in rel.parts` helper beside `is_live_plan_file`, instead of a second inline copy. Add a test with a nested archived plan.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "The transported-fact write tail is copied between import-status and retract, and the import's 'only this writer mints fact ids' comment is now false", "recommendation": "Both writers repeat the same sequence: mint `<kind>-<UTC stamp>-<uuid hex8>`, call `evidence.append_fact`, refuse `fact-append-failed`, `rev-parse HEAD`, call `evidence_notes.attach`, and print the 'does not travel to other clones' NOTE when the note was not written. A third certifying-adjacent kind would copy it again. Fix (scope: instance): one hook helper, e.g. `_append_transported_fact(kind, body)`, that owns the sequence. At minimum, delete the exclusivity claim in `_cmd_plan_import_status`'s `import uuid  # noqa: PLC0415 \u2014 only this writer mints fact ids here` (line ~7815), which retract has falsified. Nothing reads that comment, so this is wording only.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "retraction_target re-encodes which facts certify and what each names, apart from chunk_states", "recommendation": "`chunk_states` and `retraction_target` each hold their own copy of two rules: that the certifying kinds are `review` and `chunk-imported`, and how each names its chunks (`fact_chunks(body)` for a review, `body['chunk']` for an import). Today the two copies agree. If a certifying kind is added to `chunk_states` and not to the tuple in `retraction_target`, `plan retract` refuses `unknown-fact` for a fact that does certify the chunk. That fails closed, hence NOTE. Fix (scope: instance): one private helper, e.g. `_certified_chunks(fact) -> list[str] | None`, that both functions call.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "A retraction's reason always outranks a later review's blocker for the same chunk", "recommendation": "`note()` keeps the first reason written. `withdrawn()` writes the retraction reason while candidates are being collected, which is before any blocked or unreachable notes are written. So when a newer review B names a retracted chunk and is blocked, `plan status` shows \"review A names it, but it was retracted\" instead of B's open blocking finding, which is the step that actually reopens the chunk. This is two signals in one function, so it is borderline. Fix (scope: instance): either let a live reason from a later fact replace a retraction reason, or note retractions only after the review pass leaves the chunk open with no other reason. Pin the precedence with a test.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "The prose describing the proposal calls it 'built', the term for built_report's rule that the proposal rejects", "recommendation": "In `plan_status`, \"built\" is `built_report`'s term: a subject names the chunk, or its Deliverables were touched anywhere on the branch. `propose_chunks` says it never uses that rule. Yet four descriptions of the proposal use the word: `critic_consolidate.begin_review` (\"the open chunks the plan's branch has built\"), `critic_mode.boundary_review_on_chain` (\"names every chunk built and open\"), `critic_mode.short_plan_next_step` (\"names every open chunk that is built\"), and SKILL.md step 4 (\"the open chunks `plan_status.propose_chunks` finds built for this mode\"). SKILL.md's `chunk` mode also does no 'built' test at all. The plan's norm reconciliation says these surfaces point to `propose_chunks` rather than restate it, but they paraphrase it in the rejected vocabulary. A maintainer who takes them at their word could swap in `built_chunks`, which is the Chunk 01 blocker coming back. Nothing reads this prose, hence NOTE. Fix (scope: class; search descriptions of `chunks_proposed`/`propose_chunks` under `plugin/lib` and `plugin/skills` for \"built\"): make each one relational, e.g. \"the set `propose_chunks` gives for this mode\", and drop the paraphrase.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "subject_named_chunks drops the walk's cause, so a degraded whole-plan proposal looks like a complete one", "recommendation": "`_walk_branch` returns a cause string when git or the plan cannot answer (for example a declared `base:` that names no ref in this clone, a shallow clone, or a failed `git log`). `built_report` keeps that cause as `unavailable`. The new caller `subject_named_chunks` turns it into `set()`, and neither `propose_chunks` nor `critic_consolidate._proposed_chunks`/`begin_review` reports anything. The fallback direction is deliberate and right: naming fewer chunks fails closed. The problem is only that it gives no diagnostic. Consequence: at a boundary `cumulative`, a built chunk that is named only by its commit subject (one whose Deliverables name no path, which `short_plan_next_step` tells builders to make readable this way) silently drops out of `chunks_proposed`. The plan then never finishes and nothing says why. A builder who passes `--chunks` to include it gets begin_review's 'declared chunk(s) ... are not in the proposal' WARNING, which reads as overreach when the real cause is that git could not answer. Learning (hook-surface): 'Pick each fallback's direction from the invariant it protects: ... degraded advice names its consequence, and a swallow is fixed in the frame that discards it'. Fix: return the cause from `subject_named_chunks` (or a small result type from `propose_chunks`), and have begin_review append a NOTE to `notes` saying the subject names could not be read and why. Add one test where the declared base does not resolve and the dispatch notes carry the cause. Scope: instance. `_walk_branch` has one swallowing caller, and `built_report` already carries its cause.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions and flagged inferences, for the owner", "recommendation": "Scope: none. The plan records these mid-build decisions: (1) whole-plan modes add only subject-named chunks, not `built_report`'s path rule (settled by the Chunk 01 review's blocking repro; #1075 tracks the residue); (2) no planning.md edit was needed for Chunk 01; (3) Chunk 01 was dispatched with `--chunks 01` because of the #1075 overlap; (4) `plan retract` uses the import-status guard posture (refused in a scratch worktree, outside the skew guard). One decision is not listed: the Chunk 02 Deliverable specifies `plan retract <chunk> --fact <id> --reason <text>`, but the shipped command makes `--fact` optional and defaults it to the fact certifying the chunk now. The change-log, api-contract.md and the hook help all record the optional form, so nothing misleads; it is simply absent from the Decisions list. The decision-worthy fork in the inferences flagged for this boundary review is that a retraction needs no owner authority because it can only reopen a chunk. I checked it against the code: `chunk_states` only ever removes a (fact, chunk) pair from certification, and `is_finished`/`binding_progress` in critic_mode read that state, so a retraction can arm a gate and never disarm one. It is consistent with data-model.md's ledger norm as the plan cites it, but it widens who may write a governance fact, so it is the one inference worth an explicit owner confirm. The other inferences (per-pair targeting, a transported observational kind, box-import refusal, diff-only fallback) match the code and the artifacts. The plan's Goals carry no hedged or inferred goal, and every Success bullet is reached by the code.", "fid": "R-12"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings Cross-Check", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Read core.md, authoring.md, gates.md, hook-surface.md, release.md, reviews.md and tests.md (the `learnings-files --for-diff` list). This diff adds or changes no rule. One finding rests on a rule: hook-surface 'Pick each fallback's direction from the invariant it protects', cited in the subject_named_chunks finding. Checked with no regression: hook-surface 'A new op whose NAME EXTENDS an existing one silently widens every prefix-matching Bash grant'. No skill grants `plan re*` or `plan:*`; the existing grants are `plan resolve*`, `plan import-status*` and `plan check-claims*`, and none of them admits `plan retract`. hook-surface 'When a change redefines a FIELD, enumerate its READERS': `chunks_proposed`'s readers (begin_review's outside-proposal warning, the review fact body, `fact_chunks`) are unaffected in kind. Only the set got narrower, and that is the intent. core 'A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees': a sweep for 'every open chunk', 'names every chunk' and 'closes them all' finds only relational survivors (critic_mode docstrings now say 'built and open'; planning.md:156 says 'every chunk it covers'). release 'A change-log entry's BODY must cover every chunk it shipped': the fragment covers both chunks. I found no implicit supersession in the corpus.", "fid": "R-13"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog item appears resolved: brookstalley/prawduct#1072 'governance: a mid-plan final review certifies every unbuilt chunk'", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Verify it, then call `/prawduct:backlog update brookstalley/prawduct#1072 status=shipped closed-by=scope:final-review-chunks` when the backlog skill's 'When to mark shipped' rule says. Both acceptance criteria the plan cites have code behind them: the whole-plan proposal in `propose_chunks`, and `plan retract` with `chunk-retracted`. The change fragment already declares `closes: [brookstalley/prawduct#1072]`.", "fid": "R-14"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog hygiene: new item #1075 in a crowded area; other open items affecting the changed files", "recommendation": "Scope: none. C-B2: brookstalley/prawduct#1075 ('plan new's fragment stub reads as the last chunk built') was filed during this work into area `governance`, which has 33 open items. Check it for overlap with the existing governance items, especially #1055 (chunk-status compatibility shims), using `/prawduct:backlog dedup`. Its metadata is complete, so C-B1 is clear. C-B3: `affecting` over the changed files also returns #672 (coverage composes by tree but gates key on identity), #928 (host adapters) and #937 (base-advance diagnosis per Stop). This work does not touch their substance and no chunk updated them, so assess and leave as is. C-B4: every cited id (#1072, #1075, #1051) resolves.", "fid": "R-15"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Records Pass: the seven oracle records clear no bar", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Covered set (the manifest's `files_oracle`): .prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md, architecture.md, boundary-patterns.md, build-plan-final-review-chunks.md, data-model.md, security-model.md, and .prawduct/changes/final-review-chunks.md. The change fragment ships as the release note. I checked its claims against the code and found them accurate: the refusal codes match `RetractionTarget`, `--fact` is optional, the command is refused in a scratch worktree, `plan status` shows the certifying fact id, and the two renamed tests no longer exist under their old names. The api-contract.md `plan retract` entry matches `_cmd_plan_retract`'s exit codes, refusals and JSON keys. Below every bar, and no edit wanted: the plan's Chunk 02 Deliverable shows `--fact <id>` as required, and its Chunk 01 Deliverable and second Inference still say `built_report`. Both are superseded by the recorded mid-build decision and the shipped help, and following them still works. architecture.md calls `chunk-retracted` one of the 'verdict-bearing kinds' when it is observational, a phrasing it inherits from `chunk-imported`. Nothing reads either sentence to act.", "fid": "R-16"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 5, "note": 11}, "observations": [], "duration_seconds": 660, "scope": "final-review-chunks", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["02"], "chunks_proposed": ["02"], "chunk": "02", "base_reviewed": "4716fcc3e78f1ce565689714d2166cf892b1f351", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/boundary-patterns.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-final-review-chunks.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/security-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/final-review-chunks.md", "plugin/methodology/planning.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md"], "chunk_graded": "02", "chunks_graded": ["02"], "plan_graded": ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-final-review-chunks.md", "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": 0, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learnings-area-dead": 0, "change-fragment": 0, "retired-claim-survives": 0}}, "dispatched_at": "2026-10-